      "type": "text",
      "text": "\n\nGood question. In Amplifier's bundle pattern, there are two natural places to put domain context 
that an agent needs:\n\n**Option 1: `context/` file, `@`-mentioned in the agent markdown**\n\n```\ncontext/\n  
aggeus-protocol.md    <-- you write the protocol details here\nagents/\n  market-maker.md       <-- body includes: 
@utxo-bundle:context/aggeus-protocol.md\n```\n\nThis is the pattern your bundle already uses -- 
`context/instructions.md` is `@`-mentioned in `bundle.md`. The difference is you'd `@`-mention the protocol context 
inside the agent file itself, so only the market-maker agent loads it (not the root session or other agents). That's 
the **context sink** pattern -- heavy domain knowledge lives in the agent's context, not the parent 
session.\n\n**Option 2: Inline in the agent's markdown body**\n\nJust write the protocol details directly in 
`agents/market-maker.md` below the frontmatter. Simpler, but the content can't be shared with other agents or the root 
session if needed later.\n\n**Recommendation:** Option 1 -- create `context/aggeus-protocol.md` with the protocol spec,
then `@`-mention it in the market-maker agent. This keeps the agent file focused on behavior/workflow instructions 
while the protocol knowledge lives in a reusable context file. If you later build a read-only `market-analyst` agent, 
it can `@`-mention the same context file.\n\nSo the structure would be:\n\n```\ncontext/\n  instructions.md           
<-- existing (Bitcoin + LND + Aggeus tool guidance)\n  aggeus-protocol.md        <-- NEW: Aggeus protocol spec, market 
mechanics, etc.\nagents/\n  wallet-manager.md\n  lightning-specialist.md\n  market-maker.md           <-- 
@utxo-bundle:context/aggeus-protocol.md\n```\n\nWant me to scaffold `context/aggeus-protocol.md` with section headings 
for you to fill in, and then write the `market-maker.md` agent that references it?"
    }
  ],
